  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe adopt a multi-phase optimization strategy combining adaptive optimization and response surface methodology to tackle the non-convex Erds minimum overlap problem. The approach involves:\n- An initial coarse global phase using a simplified cost function to identify promising solutions\n- Refinement using gradient-based optimization\n- Local search symmetry exploitation\n- Cost-function guided proximity search\n\nThe core rationale is that the overlap integral can be significantly reduced by exploiting symmetry while maintaining integral constraints. Our method begins with an initial construction that approximates a step-function grid distribution, then optimizes using both gradient information and simulated annealing variants to cover the non-convex landscape. Key improvements include:\n- Problem-specific cost decomposition to address different time scales\n- Symmetry breaking and reconstruction mechanisms\n- Budget-adaptive parameter tuning\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nThe optimization occurs over variables h_i in [0,1] for i=1..n with integral constraint \u2211h_i = n/2. The objective is:\n\nC = max_{k} (dx) * \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n\nwhere dx=2/n, and the maximum is taken over all shifts within one time period.\n\nThe cost function is represented in discrete form by:\n- Treating shifts as difference indices k=-n+2 to n-1\n- Computing the discrete correlation: np.correlate(h,1-h,\"full\")\n- Scaling by dx\n\nConstraints are handled through:\n- Integral projection: scaling h AFTER optimization\n- Projection to [0,1] via sorting and clamping\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nParent validation and initialization:\n- Start from provided parent if available (95 samples)\n- Check integral constraint explicitly\n\nCore algorithm phases:\n\nPhase 1: Cost-profiling (coarse)\n1.1 Vectorized coarse cost approximation:\n  - Compute all shift correlations using Fast Fourier Transform\n  - Find approximate maxima with local peaks detection\n  - Return top 5 candidate shifts\n\nPhase 2: Adaptable optimization\n2.1 Cost scaling and projection:\n  - Scale h to minimize dx*variance(1-h)\n  - Project to integral=1 via total adjustment\n  - Enforce bounds 0<=h<=1\n\n2.2 Optimization using Lorentzian cost weighting:\n  - Weight cost by Lorentzian: 1/(1+w^2 * shift^2) to focus on small k\n  - Gradient descent with line-search using BFGS\n  - Penalize boundary states\n\nPhase 3: Symmetry-aware refinement\n3.1 Symmetry detection: \n  - Compute Fourier transforms for autocorrelation\n  - Detect high-period patterns\n  - Create symmetrized versions if found\n\n3.2 Line-search with restarts:\n  - Use grid search for best periods\n  - Apply period-constrained Nelder-Mead optimization\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nCooling schedule variations:\n- Simulated annealing with variable temperature\n- Tabu search with history length adaptive to plateau\n- Dynamic ranging (wide\u2192narrow)\n\nConstraint handling methods:\n- Initial feasible point: generate local perturbations\n- Narrow search regions around boundaries\n- Projection after rejection (wrap around)\n\nStopping conditions:\n- Iterative time threshold (1100 seconds)\n- Lack of improvement plateau detection\n- Cost convergence criteria:\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nDominant computations:\n- FFT-based correlations: O(n log n)\n- Gradient computations: O(n\u00b2) worst-case\n- Local adaptation: O(n empirical)\n\nEstimated evaluation time per state: ~0.015 seconds\nMaximum feasible evaluations: floor(1100/0.015) \u2248 73333\n\nCooling schedule: \n- Initial acceptance rate higher (1.5)\n- Adaptable temperature decay (log_climb)\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nValidation steps:\n- Compute C via double-check\n- Ensure integral constraint (tol 1e-4)\n- Value bounds 0<=h<=1\n- Correlation mode=\"full\" check\n\nFinal output format:\n- h_values: validated array (length n)\n- C_5: (2/n)*max(np.correlate(h,1-h,\"full\"))\n- n_points: parent length (95)\n\nStatistical validation:\n- Null hypothesis: solution not improved by >0.01\n- Tail rejection strategy\n- Multiple candidate caching ahead of time\n- Evaluation by re-measuring cost",
